riously, regarded as know-it-all, has a hot temper.\n\nWe need to consider criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nLet's assess each:\n\nA: good anticipation, complex connections, spatial vision, leadership => technical competence, decision quality, responsibility. But show-off, not open to new ideas => concerns for cooperation, adaptability, communication.\n\nB: conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), good weather assessment (technical), computer skills (technical), weak memory for numbers (attention), nasty remarks (crew cooperation, communication). So strong reliability and stress but poor teamwork.\n\nC: quick correct decisions (decision quality), calm in crisis (stress), understands complicated tech (technical), difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education (adaptability). So strong decision and stress but poor cooperation and adaptability.\n\nD: responds adequately to unexpected events (stress), concentration (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), know-it-all (cooperation?), hot temper (crew cooperation, communication). So mixed.\n\nWe need best overall fit, balancing strengths and concerns. Which candidate has least severe concerns? B has nasty remarks - serious for crew cooperation. C has egocentric and communication difficulty, also not willing to further education. D has hot temper and know-it-all attitude. A has show-off and not open to new ideas.\n\nAll have some concerns. Which is most manageable? Perhaps B's nasty remarks could be a red flag for crew safety. A's not open to new ideas could affect adaptability. C's egocentric and unwillingness to learn is concerning for long-term development. D's hot temper is serious under pressure.\n\nMaybe B's conscientiousness and stress handling outweigh nasty remarks? But nasty remarks could affect crew cooperation severely. Might be disqualifying.\n\nA's leadership and anticipation are strong for long-distance, but show-off could affect crew dynamics, but maybe less severe than nasty remarks? Not open to new ideas could hinder adaptability.\n\nC's calmness and quick decisions are valuable, but communication difficulty and egocentric could be dangerous.\n\nD's responsibility and problem solving are good, but hot temper is serious under stress.\n\nWe need to pick one. I think Candidate A may be best overall: strong technical, leadership, anticipation, but concerns about openness and show-off. Could be mitigated with training. Others have more severe interpersonal issues.\n\nThus I will recommend Candidate A.\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE: state recommendation, refer to no prior discussion, give evidence: anticipation, complex connections, leadership, but also note concerns (show-off, not open) and argue why still best.\n\nMETADATA_JSON vote: Candidate A.\n\nProceed."}
