y, positive crew atmosphere, makes correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology. Concerns: difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education.\n\nAgent 2 must consider criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nCandidate A: strong technical competence, decision quality (anticipates danger, sees connections), leadership (crew cooperation maybe), but concerns about openness (adaptability), show-off (professional communication), unorganized (attention/accuracy). No explicit stress resilience evidence, but leadership may imply some.\n\nCandidate C: reliability (100% reliable), crew atmosphere (cooperation), decision quality (quick correct decisions), stress resilience (calm in crisis), technical competence (understands technology). Concerns: communication difficulty, egocentric (crew cooperation, professional communication), not willing to further education (adaptability). No explicit attention/accuracy but likely okay.\n\nAgent 1 and 3 argue C is best despite communication issues, can be mitigated.\n\nAgent 2 could reconsider. Need to weigh: Is C's communication difficulty a bigger risk than A's unorganized and not open to new ideas? For long-distance flights, reliability and decision quality under pressure are critical. C has proven quick correct decisions and calmness. A's anticipation is good but not proven decision execution. Also A's unorganized work style could affect attention to detail, which is crucial.\n\nThus Agent 2 might shift recommendation to Candidate C. Must provide reasoning referencing facts and prior discussion.\n\nWe need to produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE: state recommendation (maybe change to C). Refer to Agent 1 and 3's points. Use explicit facts: A's show-off, not open to new ideas, unorganized; C's 100% reliability, positive crew atmosphere, quick correct decisi